import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const timeline = [
  {
    years: '2013—2020',
    org: 'Aon Hewitt',
    title: 'Programmer Analyst → Programmer Analyst Advanced',
    stack: ['ASP.NET', 'SQL', 'JCL', 'SAS', 'mainframe'],
    note: 'Custom tooling for large-employer health exchanges, built alongside actuaries and business analysts. Converting client data into a configured, production-ready system against a deadline: business-critical batch processing, legacy calculations moved onto scalable platforms, and the regression coverage to prove they still agreed.',
  },
  {
    years: '2020—2023',
    org: 'Charter Software',
    title: 'Software Engineer / Process Owner',
    stack: ['.NET Core', 'REST', 'integrations'],
    note: 'Modernized services and built new integrations for ASPEN, Charter’s dealer management platform, the CRM and ERP for agriculture and power-sports dealers, covering a specialty retail vertical the general-purpose platforms never served. Created and led the production support process, including triage and escalation.',
  },
  {
    years: '2023—2024',
    org: 'Nymbl Science',
    title: 'Software Engineer',
    stack: ['Xamarin/.NET MAUI', 'EF Core', 'Fivetran', 'dbt', 'Snowflake'],
    note: 'Full-stack work on a health startup, plus the technical program management nobody else held. Shipped a 0→1 women’s health feature and maintained the ETL pipeline that made onboarding funnel analysis possible in the first place.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Engineering · Sara Johnson"
  description="Eleven years writing production code, and what it changed about how I do product."
>
  <header class="head">
    <h1>Eleven years writing the code</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Yes, I speak the language: C#, SQL, JavaScript, and more. But reviewing PRs and
      understanding the codebase isn't my superpower. My superpower is that a decade of
      engineering has made me a lifelong builder. I launch fast and I learn fast.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Don't call it a career change</h2>

    <p>
      The tidy version of my story is engineer becomes product manager. The accurate version is
      that I have spent thirteen years developing a muscle for the same problems.
    </p>

    <p>
      At Aon Hewitt I converted client data into production-ready health exchange systems on a
      deadline. At Charter Software, I developed new direct-to-manufacturer integrations to get customer
      inventory into our system instantly and effortlessly.
      At Nymbl I obsessively measured and tweaked an onboarding funnel to statistically proven
      perfection. Now, at AbsenceSoft I own onboarding for a platform whose customers arrive carrying
      years of data from the system they are leaving.
    </p>
    <p>
      Data integration is my bread and time-to-value is
      my butter. The first eleven years I found solutions up-close by writing the code; now I find the
      right problems and decide what gets built. The move into product was zooming out, not starting over.
    </p>

    <h2>What it actually buys</h2>

    <h3>I read the code, not the documentation</h3>

    <p>
      When I built the knowledge base for our onboarding agent, I sourced it from downstream code
      behavior rather than from our own docs. The documentation described what we meant. The code
      described what would actually happen. Those are different, and the gap between them is where
      onboarding failures live.
    </p>

    <h3>I can write a spec precise enough to build from</h3>

    <p>
      Translating five years of loose Swagger into strict OpenAPI meant deciding whether a payload
      was <code>allOf</code> or <code>anyOf</code>, and whether a field was an integer or an
      enumerated integer. Those look like formatting questions and are not: they change what the
      spec promises a consumer, and a wrong guess produces confident, incorrect client code in
      someone else's repository. I made those calls directly instead of routing each one to an
      engineer. They became the company's standard for API linting.
    </p>

    <h3>I build the pilot when the argument stalls</h3>

    <p>
      Our agentic import tool sat parked in ideation for 2027, behind committed roadmap work and a
      hard resourcing limit, with very little proof of feasibility. Waiting for certainty meant
      waiting a year. So I built the pilot: the agent loop, the validation and dry-run APIs, and
      the privacy gates that had to come first, because the data is PII and PHI and what reaches a
      probabilistic evaluation needs to be deterministically bounded.
    </p>

    <p>
      Watching it run taught us something I would not have reasoned my way to in a document: some
      mapping steps were being handled identically every single time, which meant they were rules
      and did not need a model at all. We moved those into deterministic processing and left the
      agent only the ambiguous decisions. That became the team's pattern for agentic work.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it leads me wrong</h2>

    <p class="todo">
      TODO: the honest counterweight. A time the engineering instinct made me a worse product
      manager: over-indexing on elegance, solving it myself when the team should have owned it, or
      anchoring the room on my own estimate. Without this section the page is a sales pitch.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the line is now</h2>

    <p class="todo">
      TODO: do I still write code, read PRs, prototype? What do I deliberately not do anymore, and
      how do I keep from stepping on my engineers? Also: why product, and what triggered the move.
    </p>

    <h2>The record</h2>

    <ol class="timeline">
      {
        timeline.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <div class="tl-head">
              <span class="meta tl-years">{item.years}</span>
              <div>
                <p class="tl-org">{item.org}</p>
                <p class="meta tl-title">{item.title}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="tl-note">{item.note}</p>
            <div class="tl-stack">
              {item.stack.map((s) => (
                <span class="chip">{s}</span>
              ))}
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </div>
</Base>

